#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087540 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "8ba9598f-a266-57cb-8ef5-9757dd612a34" :question/math-academy-id "e-6607" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm {d} ^ 2 y} {\\textrm {d} x ^ 2} -4 \\dfrac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} +4 y = 8x + 4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 4 = 0\n$$\n\nhas a repeated root $\\lambda =  2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $1,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $1.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4\\alpha +4(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =8x+4 \\\\ 4\\alpha x+(4\\beta -4\\alpha ) & =8x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 4\\beta -4\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=2, \\beta=3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p$ is\n\n$$\ny_p =2x+3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+2x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087541 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "1b78a582-0bfa-53dc-b392-99b7da223ff6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6608" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = 1,-2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $2,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $2.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = 2\\alpha x\n+\\beta,\\qquad y_p'' = 2\\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3 \\\\ -2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\beta=-3, \\gamma=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =- \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** We write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087548 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "83788240-c717-5e86-8753-ca320f4c979a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6721" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm {d} ^ 2 y} {\\textrm {d} x ^ 2} - \\dfrac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y = 6.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = -1, 2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ {- x} + B e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a constant, it is a polynomial of degree $0,$ which means a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $0,$ or a constant. So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be found. Calculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-0-2\\alpha =6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = -3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087552 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "25b04b8f-7447-572f-8600-9d11e5423c2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6786" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x} - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3,\n\\qquad y(0) =  -\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\ \\ y'(0) = 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B -\\dfrac{1}{2}  = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = A e^{x} -2 B e^{-2 x} -x -3,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute $y'(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\nA-2B -3 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ A-2B=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1, B=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{x} -e^{-2 x}  - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81cc546e-6ee9-581c-a55e-9944c5ff8e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-x^{2} + 2x + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085922} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6efea9-2618-568a-8b66-4b1178d563cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = 2x^{2} - x + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085923} {:answer/id #uuid "1e978853-3633-5fe0-98f4-32dfa6beba5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-x^{2} + 3x - 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085924} {:answer/id #uuid "24085b70-9d5a-5784-8a9c-0af91c78a5fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = x^{2} + x + 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085925} {:answer/id #uuid "7da978a5-3be4-5434-988a-7dd5d8829a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = x^{2} - 3x - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81cc546e-6ee9-581c-a55e-9944c5ff8e2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-x^{2} + 2x + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085922} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dac09d1-080f-56f1-a3f4-362ae1f4d608" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43efe1d0-c638-55a1-8bd9-f43f26e2bc24" :question/math-academy-id "q-64206" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-6x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha -12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma )=12{x}^{2}-32x-30\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-12\\alpha =12\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha -12\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta -12\\gamma =-30\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32fbf00c-e271-5865-a47f-dc7db54f192f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{x}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349529} {:answer/id #uuid "be31ed43-cd2f-5ea8-a547-2547eaaf5b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349530} {:answer/id #uuid "2383eb63-78b9-59bb-a6cf-709331f3508d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349531} {:answer/id #uuid "9346bdbc-9a23-5d8a-b079-02bfb56d9557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349532} {:answer/id #uuid "608a9e08-fbc2-57aa-af12-f7d43b530bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9346bdbc-9a23-5d8a-b079-02bfb56d9557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349532} :answer-field/id #uuid "94dfcbb3-2a5c-5e2c-85ef-2079e2a27601" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d511e4f-8803-5021-b5c2-53a7b4de5acf" :question/math-academy-id "q-64285" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y=-5x+6\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha -5(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-5\\alpha x+(-4\\alpha -5\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -4\\alpha -5\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d447568-45b8-5b7c-8832-0f6d851ef316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349536} {:answer/id #uuid "d55dc3e8-72e1-57dc-96a7-88babd6a6ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349537} {:answer/id #uuid "08c9aef4-21e2-5286-b9da-f008dbd978c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}+4x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349538} {:answer/id #uuid "2be27aa9-ef75-565e-b292-c7f21b79b5c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+5x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349539} {:answer/id #uuid "79c79637-df5f-5809-9e49-8288f6e33f8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+3x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d55dc3e8-72e1-57dc-96a7-88babd6a6ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349537} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e6bda93-ff1b-5f34-b8be-63689be1468f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd702971-4dc4-5e03-9b53-1243b162ebed" :question/math-academy-id "q-77763" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -3(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\beta -6\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\beta -6\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =6,\\,\\gamma =6.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148c5c16-2241-5c67-9a75-598f367b0107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349543} {:answer/id #uuid "a06f759d-32e0-56f8-8dd0-8f0dad8184d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349544} {:answer/id #uuid "18be48d5-dd34-58dd-93c0-a62de2eda03c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-2x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349545} {:answer/id #uuid "53bdafdd-558a-5c66-a4d0-e29a1a25f254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349546} {:answer/id #uuid "3c4bbd75-ea77-5dcb-8f35-634361045560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c4bbd75-ea77-5dcb-8f35-634361045560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349547} :answer-field/id #uuid "79c8147f-4ebd-5c45-8a45-a9c70343d484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2fba9ca-a181-507b-8602-6d18bc6141a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-7896" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=12.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =2$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0-6\\alpha =12\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7164818-4a5d-5bd5-934b-d970dc128da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349550} {:answer/id #uuid "245f28f3-1ddd-502d-8462-377f891489a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349551} {:answer/id #uuid "44552cd7-b0d7-581f-831c-897c0eeecf60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349552} {:answer/id #uuid "f2883814-5631-50c1-b466-f7456529b7d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-x}-x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349553} {:answer/id #uuid "1c28ddac-c82d-56d7-b283-a4e4c25e6372" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-x}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "245f28f3-1ddd-502d-8462-377f891489a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349551} :answer-field/id #uuid "51147bac-3264-5105-8f73-eeb85cb5d7e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "954ee000-a265-58c3-9263-7daaff9ae9c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-7897" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=3x+1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =1$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+2\\alpha -3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=3x+1\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-3\\alpha x+(2\\alpha -3\\beta )=3x+1.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -3\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}-x-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088307 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b047b92d-2619-5f4e-bd83-d39ca9e6f627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-3x} + 4e^{3x} + \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086773} {:answer/id #uuid "b65a206e-d9ab-5510-87d7-c8dda4feef10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-3x} + 8xe^{-3x} - \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086774} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ae66a5-1c8d-5eb6-bcb2-3317d23df03e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-3x} + 8xe^{-3x} + \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086775} {:answer/id #uuid "feaf295f-1529-5702-b2b3-3a238404e82e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2e^{-3x} + e^{3x} - \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086776} {:answer/id #uuid "75af004e-d418-5a83-8309-7f63dc4c9309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2e^{-3x} + 4xe^{-3x} + \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7ae66a5-1c8d-5eb6-bcb2-3317d23df03e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-3x} + 8xe^{-3x} + \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086775} :answer-field/id #uuid "6878f0cf-a714-5bce-bdd0-ab530b37faf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "870e7b8e-206b-50c2-8d89-7f75a9431965" :question/math-academy-id "q-7901" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=3,\\,y(0)=\\frac{4}{3},\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=0,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+9\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{4}{3}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, $A=1.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(B-3A){e}^{-3x}-3Bx{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\nB-3A=5.\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1$ and $B=8.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d3b350-fd5a-56d8-a44d-0d6d5e1ee224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-4x} + Bxe^{-4x} - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086779} {:answer/id #uuid "224b95ec-61c5-5ec8-b2ce-39ddfd92282f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-4x} + Bxe^{-4x} + 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086780} {:answer/id #uuid "359f4f7f-a6fd-5357-aa6b-d2d467a638c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-4x} + Bxe^{-4x} - x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086781} {:answer/id #uuid "719fa65b-250a-530d-8cff-2b2feafc9ab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4x} + Be^{-4x} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086782} {:answer/id #uuid "7d476e59-59e2-5d1f-ba65-d9384d319fda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{4x} + Be^{-4x} - x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "224b95ec-61c5-5ec8-b2ce-39ddfd92282f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-4x} + Bxe^{-4x} + 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086780} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c213798-9c40-5ba0-92fd-1fb8f0453923" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "513cfc62-eb9a-585a-a155-a58861456868" :question/math-academy-id "q-7902" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=32x+16.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha +16(\\alpha x+\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n16\\alpha x+(8\\alpha +16\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha =32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 8\\alpha +16\\beta =16\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "965b0c34-dd90-52f5-bfe7-53328659755f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{5x} + Bxe^{5x} + x^{2} + 2x + \\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086785} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f4e0a4-6430-588c-8f71-7a8936693ce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{5x} + Be^{-5x} + x^{2} + x + \\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086786} {:answer/id #uuid "bae548e9-2685-559b-9834-621971932bbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{5x} + Bxe^{5x} + x^{2} + 2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086787} {:answer/id #uuid "05ea333b-67a5-5456-bbb9-d13e0f29e8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{5x} + Bxe^{5x} + x^{2} + x + \\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086788} {:answer/id #uuid "2da9bcaf-cd2f-52fe-ab33-69ebb7cd54ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{5x} + Be^{-5x} + x^{2} + x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05ea333b-67a5-5456-bbb9-d13e0f29e8db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{5x} + Bxe^{5x} + x^{2} + x + \\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086788} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d06a790-b638-5f2e-8d13-2f095b846237" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "445996cc-ad9d-514f-8bb7-d053c9192103" :question/math-academy-id "q-7903" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=25{x}^{2}+5x-3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =5$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -10(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+25(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n25\\alpha {x}^{2}+(25\\beta -20\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}25\\alpha =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 25\\beta -20\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d83ef38-e818-5e95-a2c8-0b75bbf23941" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349557} {:answer/id #uuid "105ff811-c432-5482-9056-8bbfa0646e30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349558} {:answer/id #uuid "1be3491e-6126-5b7e-9b24-6b2dd791eb35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349559} {:answer/id #uuid "15cf578a-442e-545a-9dd8-c81e00f86e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349560} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd71c86-d57b-5c64-8e81-16e29f15cc6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfd71c86-d57b-5c64-8e81-16e29f15cc6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349561} :answer-field/id #uuid "054f6bc4-e22f-5eab-a5fe-05eaf8e96d74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b95c671b-321a-50e6-94b2-76f7485353a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-7904" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=-4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+16\\alpha =-4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80912605-c6cc-5f8e-8e00-82e20cf1ce7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{x}(\\cos 5x + 2\\sin 5x) + \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + 2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086881} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1697a4-5836-5f95-802b-9bf061d44044" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-x}(\\cos 3x + 2\\sin 3x) + \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + 2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086882} {:answer/id #uuid "4b59427e-4aea-5087-bf10-1025419e8f75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{x}(\\cos 5x - \\sin 5x) + \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{x}{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086883} {:answer/id #uuid "60b53065-eeea-5c83-b244-ad99791c7a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-x}(\\cos 3x - \\sin 3x) + x^{2} + \\frac{x}{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086884} {:answer/id #uuid "70da8c2f-3e99-5aba-a213-a58c94d17fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-x}(3\\cos 5x + \\sin 5x) + \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{x}{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70da8c2f-3e99-5aba-a213-a58c94d17fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = e^{-x}(3\\cos 5x + \\sin 5x) + \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{x}{2} - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086885} :answer-field/id #uuid "56050120-c4fd-5d4f-8b91-9ee3a7579d7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddb29a9d-408f-5d53-82cb-356f77e1a234" :question/math-academy-id "q-8034" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=13{x}^{2}+15x-24,\\,y(0)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+26 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral, ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+26(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n26\\alpha {x}^{2}+(26\\beta +4\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}26\\alpha =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 26\\beta +4\\alpha =15\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma =-24\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =\\frac{1}{2},\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$  \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP-1=2.\n$$\n\nThus, $P=3.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{-x}[(5Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-(5P+Q)\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x]+x+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n5Q-P+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=3$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0dc5d18-6e76-5584-b7c9-3f75619e489f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349564} {:answer/id #uuid "592eeb51-4247-50d9-9280-ae13bc2e431d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349565} {:answer/id #uuid "0c4aedbc-85dd-57e4-9f28-92457f9dcf1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349566} {:answer/id #uuid "91c96f37-819b-52ef-a7ae-c558be6d35d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349567} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3a96bf-2007-5760-9224-3a935ce21038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d3a96bf-2007-5760-9224-3a935ce21038" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349568} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ecebbc-de9e-5e0f-a98d-d286c988b214" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f079ad1b-3fa4-5b05-93e6-15c915d68e55" :question/math-academy-id "q-93668" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=20\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x),$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+5\\alpha =20\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "734ec4ef-42ae-5de9-9117-5cf6638ef440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349571} {:answer/id #uuid "0c70f383-0715-5c8b-a3f3-9df7ff8f736f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349572} {:answer/id #uuid "09e21cb6-826c-5baf-a418-c1a2e0363f67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349573} {:answer/id #uuid "5d25de67-1e4a-59f1-8484-f49fe39593c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349574} {:answer/id #uuid "655de9ea-d8fd-5ec3-9bdf-3ea2154fbed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "655de9ea-d8fd-5ec3-9bdf-3ea2154fbed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349575} :answer-field/id #uuid "56fb338c-93a1-58b8-8ae9-a76c71bac543" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e3900ba-b694-5263-b920-c3f6f1480f74" :question/math-academy-id "q-93669" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =2$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0-8\\alpha =4\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69619610-e793-5c12-91fd-83a133f5188e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349578} {:answer/id #uuid "19937a44-a0ff-511a-be4a-3514548e5bf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349579} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5b057c-bbb7-5248-97ac-136a7cc077b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349580} {:answer/id #uuid "dbea8af9-499a-5e6b-baa4-f26e3331585b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349581} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbf711b-4858-5f16-b5cf-f68396e1a314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bbf711b-4858-5f16-b5cf-f68396e1a314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349582} :answer-field/id #uuid "304b2665-d148-54cd-bf73-9d35f5996a59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7a0a468-1c88-521a-84b2-9f5ade22295f" :question/math-academy-id "q-93672" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+9\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349583 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0348ac0-bb22-51ad-9366-dfec16525369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{3x}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349585} {:answer/id #uuid "09adaf32-b70d-5020-a894-c96bef42b425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349586} {:answer/id #uuid "507b9e22-3723-59b7-bb99-0dfa492551b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349587} {:answer/id #uuid "17eaa144-855c-579f-958e-69ada1212473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349588} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd39c46-3b81-5294-800c-eba62f77e67b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17eaa144-855c-579f-958e-69ada1212473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349588} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fc01c58-48df-54e4-bbfc-30e7e3ee11ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d71200d-b565-5b6c-93a9-0d26e985c2e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-93811" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=18x+3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =3,$ and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-\\alpha -6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=18x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha x+(-\\alpha -6\\beta )=18x+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =18\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -\\alpha -6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bc7096e-d78c-5c37-8faf-54b050328eab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349592} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2cc807-bec3-54a6-99ec-0cdab4c31f5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}-\\frac{x}{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349593} {:answer/id #uuid "0cdca85f-8cb5-5a40-b972-72d608d9ea94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)-\\frac{x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349594} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b35ce3-393a-533a-a362-d3edbb331cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349595} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a7e6a6-03f3-52ed-becd-e4e5231f4059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}-2x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f2cc807-bec3-54a6-99ec-0cdab4c31f5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}-\\frac{x}{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349593} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d78200f-a454-56ad-b022-d0acba8b963c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2c91d5d-1887-52e2-b19f-c23ea4f54318" :question/math-academy-id "q-93812" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-16y=8x-32.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =4$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-16(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x-32\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-16\\alpha x-16\\beta =8x-32.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-16\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -16\\beta =-32\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{x}{2}+2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{4x}-\\frac{x}{2}+2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "658f02df-e117-5123-80a9-8d04dccc18c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2{x}^{2}+4x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349599} {:answer/id #uuid "5100ebc5-35d5-594f-9fab-2af88a2a55ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349600} {:answer/id #uuid "27bb4e02-615a-52e9-b0cb-f5c23240e81c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}={x}^{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349601} {:answer/id #uuid "297d6fee-79b4-528b-87cf-3763cfc5ccd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2x-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349602} {:answer/id #uuid "cf003240-fe18-5ab6-8f6a-7cd87dca714a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2{x}^{2}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5100ebc5-35d5-594f-9fab-2af88a2a55ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349600} :answer-field/id #uuid "24022750-ee7b-5d8f-9ce8-77c3c27a8697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f5f62bb-4839-53ca-a27c-2aa63221765a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93827" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+Bx{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+4(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha +4\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta +4\\gamma )=-2{x}^{2}-4x+3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha +4\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta +4\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ce0bb76-fd3b-5d3c-be7e-037c818dd4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349606} {:answer/id #uuid "41bb8d40-e774-5ff5-b966-37eacfaaf29d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}-{x}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349607} {:answer/id #uuid "1bfd82bd-902e-5a2d-a500-4d5ac0709fdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}+2x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349608} {:answer/id #uuid "5049b73b-a217-57e0-b55e-da248fb5637c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}+{x}^{2}-2x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349609} {:answer/id #uuid "afaebfdb-2306-5f42-9e4a-4be41e2e138d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ce0bb76-fd3b-5d3c-be7e-037c818dd4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349606} :answer-field/id #uuid "59019122-b9a7-598f-a15f-7e1535e654c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffa40815-fb21-52fd-a6a4-4ce989aef44c" :question/math-academy-id "q-93830" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=24{x}^{2}+4x+2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+12y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +12 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-4$ or $\\lambda =-3$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}$ is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +7(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+12(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=24{x}^{2}+4x+2\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n12\\alpha {x}^{2}+(14\\alpha +12\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +7\\beta +12\\gamma )=24{x}^{2}+4x+2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}12\\alpha =24\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 14\\alpha +12\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +7\\beta +12\\gamma =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}-2x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by \n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-3x}+2{x}^{2}-2x+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "357a16fa-ff1c-530e-97f2-a5b05fdcef3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349613} {:answer/id #uuid "eee5fc65-ec89-5a41-9530-5ac0e5ea3a5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}+2x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349614} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3cdf9b-3d66-5d51-9de6-a7fa25ff6636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349615} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac6096c-93f8-56db-9fa8-b8b9aa5b2acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}+4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349616} {:answer/id #uuid "51c78d6c-da3f-5410-ab1b-5e077c8d9137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ac6096c-93f8-56db-9fa8-b8b9aa5b2acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-4x}+4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349616} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca1551c-52cb-5cc5-a813-2ff731a48319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e8bc97a-c1b6-51d3-8b5a-21e74de39d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-94258" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=-8\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=-8,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{-4x}-4Bx{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4A+B & =0 \\\\ -4(1)+B & =0 \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-4x}+4x{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349618 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "459789aa-4181-5d3a-892f-e2624d53e0e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349620} {:answer/id #uuid "c04e5305-b37e-5b36-8f70-85112b13595e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349621} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1c9da6-b967-50bf-9702-1db746098c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349622} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc004a1-86aa-57bb-947b-7074a44e84a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349623} {:answer/id #uuid "da0700a9-6f99-53d3-b167-d1e64e8ee804" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "459789aa-4181-5d3a-892f-e2624d53e0e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349620} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a725290-09ae-545b-a24c-b3d59324f0c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fd3f68f-27a8-5cd0-8e1d-99989db25fe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-94329" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=-5{x}^{2}+x-5\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=-5{x}^{2}+x-5,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-1 & =0 \\\\ P & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}[(2Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(-Q-2P)\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]-2x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q-P+1 & =4 \\\\ 2Q-1+1 & =4 \\\\ Q & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b116b9eb-10aa-5126-9ea9-db7b0e85b841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-2x}+3{e}^{2x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349627} {:answer/id #uuid "265a4f54-42f0-546c-8f11-e24caf6c8f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-2x}-{e}^{2x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349628} {:answer/id #uuid "511e2c7e-605f-5674-bdc3-740bbd612faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{e}^{2x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349629} {:answer/id #uuid "da40b38c-1d5e-5e59-a088-3ee7966d66be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349630} {:answer/id #uuid "df55cf39-98a8-5b5a-8752-c5f8dc4c9697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-2x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da40b38c-1d5e-5e59-a088-3ee7966d66be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349630} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea13de8d-ef21-5941-9743-a6122dca0081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44df3166-ba97-53d9-8a68-878f2b446ce3" :question/math-academy-id "q-94342" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4y=8x-4\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}-2x+1,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4y=8x-4,\\,y(0)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B+1 & =4 \\\\ A+B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2A{e}^{-2x}+2B{e}^{2x}-2,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2A+2B-2 & =0 \\\\ A-B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=3 \\\\ A-B=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,B=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}+2{e}^{2x}-2x+1.\n$$"}]}
